#!/usr/bin/env bash
[[ ! ${ROLL_DIR} ]] && >&2 echo -e "\033[31mThis script is not intended to be run directly!\033[0m" && exit 1
## @description: Remove Docker images roll downloaded and no longer needs.
## @category: utility

ROLL_USAGE=$(cat <<'USAGE'
\033[33mUsage:\033[0m
  image-cleanup                    Remove superseded and unused roll images
  image-cleanup --dry-run          List what would be removed and change nothing

\033[33mOptions:\033[0m
  --dry-run         Only list the images
  -h, --help        Display this help menu

\033[33mDescription:\033[0m
  Removes two kinds of image, without asking:

  superseded  the previous build of a tag, left behind without a tag when a pull found a
              newer one. Nothing can use it again.
  unused      a tagged image no container uses, running or stopped, such as an old PHP
              version a project no longer pins. An environment taken down with roll env down
              has no containers left, so its images are removed as well and downloaded again
              on its next roll env up.

  A tag a container was created from counts as used even after a pull moved it to a newer
  build, so an environment that is up or stopped keeps its images.

  Superseded images are also removed automatically after roll svc pull, roll svc up,
  roll env pull and roll env up; unused ones only by this command. Set
  ROLL_IMAGE_AUTO_CLEANUP=0 in ~/.roll/.env to turn the automatic run off.

  Only images roll itself runs are touched: everything under ROLL_IMAGE_REPOSITORY plus the
  third-party images named in the roll compose files (traefik, mailpit, selenium and others).
  Docker refuses to remove an image a container still uses, and this command never forces it.

  The containerd image store of newer Docker Desktop installs keeps no name on a superseded
  image, so roll records the IDs of its images in ~/.roll/tmp/roll-image-ids before every pull
  and recognises them from there. An image superseded before roll recorded it is left alone.
USAGE
)
